modules.theory:
JavaScript runs on one thread. Slow work does not block: it finishes later, and a queue of callbacks runs when the current code is done.

```js
setTimeout(() => console.log("later"), 100);
```

A **Promise** is a value that arrives later.

```js
const wait = ms => new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, ms));
```

`await` pauses an `async` function until a promise settles. Rejections become exceptions:

```js
async function load() {
  try {
    await wait(10);
    return "done";
  } catch (e) { ... }
}
```

Order to remember: normal code first, then promise callbacks (microtasks), then timers.

`Promise.all(list)` waits for all and fails on the first failure. `Promise.race` takes the first to finish. `Promise.allSettled` never fails.

Your code in Codebit runs inside an async function, so you can use `await` at the top level.

