modules.theory:
## How tasks run your code

The app runs your program once for each test and types the input for it. Each read takes the **next line** of that input. Your code must read what it needs and never contain the example values.

If an example's input has two lines, your program has to read two lines.

`cin >> x` reads the next value, and it does not care whether that value is on the same line or the next one.

A C++ program starts in `main`. It is **compiled** first: the compiler checks every type before anything runs.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int a;
    string name;
    cin >> a >> name;          // reads words, skips whitespace
    cout << name << " " << a * 2 << "\n";
    return 0;
}
```

Every variable has a fixed type: `int`, `long long` (big whole numbers), `double`, `char`, `bool`, `string`. An `int` overflows beyond about 2 billion, with no warning.

`7 / 2` with ints is `3`. Use `7.0 / 2` or a cast: `(double)a / b`.

Read a whole line with `getline(cin, s)`. Read until input ends: `while (cin >> x) { ... }`

For fixed decimals: `#include <iomanip>` and `cout << fixed << setprecision(2) << x;`

Note: in Codebit, `try`, `catch` and `throw` are not available, because the phone compiler is built without exceptions.

