modules.theory:
A **template** is code written for any type. The compiler makes a version for each type you use.

```cpp
template <typename T>
T biggest(T a, T b) { return a > b ? a : b; }

template <typename T>
class Box {
    T value;
public:
    Box(T v) : value(v) {}
    T get() const { return value; }
};
```

**Smart pointers** own heap memory and free it themselves:

```cpp
#include <memory>
auto p = make_unique<Node>();   // one owner
auto q = move(p);               // ownership moves, p is now empty
auto s = make_shared<Node>();   // shared, counted
```

**Move** transfers guts rather than copying them. A class that owns memory needs care: if you write a destructor, you almost always need a copy constructor and assignment too (the Rule of Three).

