modules.theory:
## How tasks run your code

The app runs your program once for each test and types the input for it. Each read takes the **next line** of that input. Your code must read what it needs and never contain the example values.

If an example's input has two lines, your program has to read two lines.

`scanf("%d", &x)` reads the next value, and it does not care whether that value is on the same line or the next one.

C is small. Input and output use format strings.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n;
    double x;
    char word[32];
    scanf("%d %lf %31s", &n, &x, word);   // & gives scanf the address
    printf("%d %.2f %s\n", n, x, word);
    return 0;
}
```

Formats: `%d` int, `%ld` long, `%lld` long long, `%f` double in printf, `%lf` double in scanf, `%c` char, `%s` string, `%zu` size_t.

`scanf` returns how many items it read, so this reads until input ends:

```c
while (scanf("%d", &x) == 1) { ... }
```

There is no `string` or `bool` type built in. Use `#include <stdbool.h>` for `bool`. Variables are **not** set to zero for you: an uninitialised local holds garbage.

